 "drivingQuestion": "Why is a brother from a maidservant or gentile excluded?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "Derived from Exodus 21:4.",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "yevamot.22b.5",
   "daf": "22b",
   "sentenceId": 4,
   "he": "חוּץ מִמִּי שֶׁיֵּשׁ לוֹ אָח מִן הַשִּׁפְחָה וּמִן הַגּוֹיָה. מַאי טַעְמָא? אָמַר קְרָא: ״הָאִשָּׁה וִילָדֶיהָ תִּהְיֶה לַאדוֹנֶיהָ״.",
   "en": "§ The mishna says: «Except for whoever has a brother» born «from a» Canaanite «maidservant or from a gentile woman,» as in these cases he is not considered a brother. The Gemara asks: «What is the reason» for this halakha? The Gemara answers: «The verse states» with regard to a Jewish slave who has children with a Canaanite maidservant: «“The wife and her children shall be her master’s”» (Exodus 21:4). This means that the children also have the status of Canaanite slaves, which implies that the lineage of a Canaanite maidservant’s child comes from the mother and not from the father."
  }
 ],
